CONVERSATION:
greetings - just catching up on archives.
fe5fc1e9bf60926e09f6f91b4e6df403, is your WordPress plugin listed on the hResume implementations section/page? http://microformats.org/wiki/hresume#Implementations
ntoll - very cool with the hAtom support
tantek: could you check the output to see if I've marked it up right before I do? http://blog.signpostmarv.name/
go ahead and add it anyway - no need to wait.
recalls your previous opinion that &quot;virtual worlds weren't deserving of microformats&quot;- does that apply to what this plugin is doing ?
it's certainly more interesting to discuss an actual example like you have on your blog
in general the markup looks very good
looks like there are two &quot;summary&quot; elements - is that intentional?
oh ?
&lt;a class=&quot;url fn summary&quot; rel=&quot;me&quot; href=&quot;http://world.secondlife.com/resident/83b3987f-9520-4275-8efe-3ac13dd3f635&quot;&gt fe5fc1e9bf60926e09f6f91b4e6df403 Martin&lt;/a&gt;
ah, typo
&lt;p class=&quot;summary&quot;&gt fe5fc1e9bf60926e09f6f91b4e6df403 Martin - If...
i was using hListing before I switched to hResume
also, perhaps this
&lt;br /&gt;
http://onxiam.com/people fe5fc1e9bf60926e09f6f91b4e6df403&lt;/p&gt;
could be marked up
its pulled from http://world.secondlife.com/resident/83b3987f-9520-4275-8efe-3ac13dd3f635
&lt;a rel=&quot;me&quot; href=&quot;http://onxiam.com/people fe5fc1e9bf60926e09f6f91b4e6df403&quot;&gt;my OnXiam page&lt;/a&gt;
the scraper code removes the extra HTML, since there are a few bugs with regards to Linden Lab's link parsing
ah ok
international characters have a tendency to break their output
(its also entered as plain text when you edit your profile)
but yes, its a good point.
I'll put it on the to-do list
I think the only key question remaining is do you want to conflate your physical and SL identities
pseudonyms
conflicts are only likely to arise when you markup your virtual identity with physical properties
e.g. bday
ah
which I didn't really realize until seeing your actual example
(this has been a good exercise)
bday = date account was created, known colloquially as &quot;rezday&quot;
^in this case
indeed. consider that people don't list their AIM or other online accounts as having a &quot;bday&quot; of the date the account was created
my understanding of bday is that it is meant to represent the physical birth date of the entity represented by the vCard/hCard
in practice, Second Life users tend to celebrate their rezday as they would their birthday
there are other analogies for this too - such as the &quot;member since 1998&quot; printing on credit cards
the date of when that specific facet/identity was created
rather than the entity that the facet/identity is a part of
I think they can be considered different concepts
hEvent then ?
given that rezday is recognized and regarded as special in Second Life, it seems like a reasonable thing to add to http://microformats.org/wiki/vcard-suggestions#Suggestions_for_New_Properties
hmm - yes, you could mark it up as an hCalendar event as well
&lt;div class=&quot;rezday vevent&quot;&gt;&lt;span class=&quot;summary&quot;&gt;Rezday: &lt;/span&gt; &lt;abbr class=&quot;dtstart&quot; title=&quot;2005-10-08&quot;&gt;Rezday: 8th Oct, 2005&lt;/abbr&gt;
&lt;/div&gt; ?
that seems reasonable - perhaps with the &quot;:&quot; omitted from the summary (can be put outside the span), and removing the duplicate &quot;Rezday:&quot; inside the dtstart.
