69b0d3dfe919a6b860a9fac82de52a7e: how did you come up with the Sec-WebSocket-KeyX thing?
especially the part which allows dividing by 0
er
ah it is explained
still, this all sounds strange
it's pretty crazy yeah
it was hard to come up with something that's hard to screw up
not sure what you don't understand about hte origin thing... can you elaborate?
not sure which part to explain :-)
it's just saying that if you have a simple script that just expects to talk to pages hosted from one page, it can just hard-code the Sec-WebSocket-Origin response field
but if it's a more complicated script that talks to many sites on the web, it'll need to look at the Origin field from the client first
and then only echo the one that the client sent
69b0d3dfe919a6b860a9fac82de52a7e: the multiple origins part
69b0d3dfe919a6b860a9fac82de52a7e: so client sends Origin: and server sends back Sec-WebSocket-Origin:
and in some cases Origin:
which is the same as what client sent to server?
the server always sends back Sec-WebSocket-Origin
and its value is either hard-coded, or derived from the client's Origin:
why is Sec-WebSocket-Origin needed?
to make sure you can't talk to a server who doesn't want to talk to you
(and so that the client is the one doing the check, not the server)
(since we don't trust the server to get things right)
well, if also server sends Origin
server never sends Origin
Origin: is the field that describes the client's origin
oh, ok
my bad
misread
thanks
np
still, the protocol is getting far from being trivial to implement
it's still pretty simple on the server. the client isn't trivial, certainly.
the latest changes added about 50% extra complexity to the server based on my attempts to implement a perl one
