CONVERSATION:
+would you recomment bitmap on heaviliy updated OLAP table ?
+no!
+it is OLAP
+every 30 mins a lot of data is deleted/inserted
+no concurrent writes
+no users accessing the system at the same time?
+readers
+concurrent reads yes
+you'll probably have lots of commit points anyway
+no
+I prepare in separate table
+then INSERT into olap select * from temp
+if you have lots of data you should have several commit points?
+otherwise you'll suffer from loads of undo
+what you mean ?
+I have several commit points yes
+if you insert 5 million rows without any commits ... you get a significan amount of undo
+hali: yes, but I can afford it
+hali: why not
+if you do heavy updates it may even be worth disabling the index and rebuilding after
+or wait a minute
+you said updates
+selects are 24x7
+but then inserts/
+update = delete + insert
+inserts are not as bad on a bitmap index as updates
+I do not have &quot;update .. set&quot;
+I have delete -&gt; insert new
+only delete and insert statements
+you run EE right?
+yes
+so you can do an online rebuild of the index
+yes
+I can rebuild index from time to time
+but if I disable it during insert then it will not be available right ?
+disable index for insert -&gt; select can't use it while I'm inserting
+if you disable it selects will even fail
+unless you set it to accept unuable indexes
+just do it the simplest way to begin with
+if it's to slow.. fine.. change it
+every 30 min disable-rebuild index on large table..
+hurts
+don't dsiable.. just do the plain insert... with larger commit intervals
+nod
+should I make large pctfree for bitmaps ?
+may be worth to append data if you can afford the disk space
+hali.. pctfree ?
+higher than usual ... but these days it's not the end of the world
+with locally mananged tablespaces and automatic segment managment things are quite simple
+default is 10 if i remember correct.. probably fine
+I thought larger pctfree will prevent bitmaps from growing fast ?
+possibly... it depends on the distribution of data as well
+but since you are using bitmaps i presume it is ok
+but if you set it to high some blocks may not even be filled
+attention. &quot;
+pretty smart structures and it is the rare index that needs extra special attention.&quot;
+ok, me quoting tom kyte just screwed up :)
+anyway, indexes are not stupid... they don't need to much attention these days
+i mean, lots of dba's insist on fucking around with things to try to &quot;tune&quot; every little aspect.. but everything is to dynamic
+Donald Burleson is the king of missconception of these things
+or take John Weeg
+<a href="http://www.dbazine.com/oracle/or-articles/weeg16">http://www.dbazine.com/oracle/or-articles/weeg16</a>
+read that, and do exactly the opposite
+i.e. Why would you want to rebuild indexes with a lot of unused space?
+every bit counts
+;)
+the space will be reclaimed for like 10 minutes
+and then you'll have the same unused space..
+the index is not static..
+yes
+even selects will buff it around
+the only reason i can think of that would significantly improve with pctfree at say 50 is that you get less undo
+I thought about 67
+but well, I'll monitor it
+hmm
+regular online rebuilds of bitmap is considered bad also ?
+I heard that regular btree rebuild is bad and the argumentation is clear
+but what w/ bitmaps
+with bitmaps its different yes
+i did a quick test here...
+a column with 1.5million rows.... about 20 distinct values..
+...
+ACTION is all ears
+with pctfree 67 the index is 2 megs after a few million rows updates
+with pcftree 10 it is 512k
+since the updates are to small for the old blocks to be considered
