69b0d3dfe919a6b860a9fac82de52a7..." | raw="
CONVERSATION:
b8810fee2f4a71f849f3f7409546d1d9: can you give an example of what you mean?
69b0d3dfe919a6b860a9fac82de52a7e: I had an example in the email
looking it up
69b0d3dfe919a6b860a9fac82de52a7e: http:/ b8810fee2f4a71f849f3f7409546d1d9.iki.fi/test/moz/flushing-document-written-text-no-alert.html is different for you in Firefox 3.5 and IE8, right?
i meant like a tiny example :-)
is your example basically  &lt;script&gt;append a text node&lt;/script&gt;x -- are there two text nodes or one?
69b0d3dfe919a6b860a9fac82de52a7e: no, the crux is the line document.getElementById(&quot;foo&quot;).appendChild(document.createElement(&quot;span&quot;))
my confusion is with &quot;Firefox appends more text to the last text node it was appending to&quot;, since if a script is running, the last text node it inserted into was the &lt;script&gt;'s child node
you're saying you want     &lt;script&gt;write 'aaa'; append element node&lt;/script&gt;bbb  to result in &quot;aaabbb element node&quot; rather than &quot;aaa element node bbb&quot;?
i'm very confused
69b0d3dfe919a6b860a9fac82de52a7e: former in Firefox, latter in IE
well clearly imho aaabbb element is outright incorrect.
69b0d3dfe919a6b860a9fac82de52a7e: you want to spec what IE does?
WebKit is element aaabbb, IIRC
and Opera
the spec already requires the text after the &lt;script&gt; to come after whatever state the DOM is in after the script executes.
element aaa bbb is definitely wrong per spec, it means the parser isn't re-entrant.
(or acts as if it's not, which is worse)
http://www.hixie.ch/tests/adhoc/html/parsing/026-demo.html
69b0d3dfe919a6b860a9fac82de52a7e: the webkit/opera behavior flows naturally from a late-flushed accumulation buffer
webkit's behaviour is clearly wrong.
then a late-flushed accumulation buffer is a bad dea.
idea
69b0d3dfe919a6b860a9fac82de52a7e: it's 2 out of 4 :-)
I'll have to get back on this when I have a decent text input method
IE's behaviour in http://www.hixie.ch/tests/adhoc/html/parsing/026-demo.html is what the spec requires
and is the only behaviour i've found in the four browsers i tested that is even remotely sane.
anyway, as i noted in my e-mail reply, just use the text node's own buffer as the buffer
69b0d3dfe919a6b860a9fac82de52a7e: you have an interesting notion of sane
and then you get IE's behaviour for free
really? you don't think having things come out in the order they were written is sane?
69b0d3dfe919a6b860a9fac82de52a7e: nope, then you get the Firefox behavior
you only get firefox's behaviour if you don't check to make sure the text node is still the last child each time a script runs
(which is a trivial pointer test)
69b0d3dfe919a6b860a9fac82de52a7e: my independent implementation matched webkit and opera, and I like to think I'm sane
i'm not saying the implementor isn't sane
i'm saying that having nodes come out in an order different than the order they went in is unintuitive
depends on whether your intuition is buffered :-)
reload http://www.hixie.ch/tests/adhoc/html/parsing/026-demo.html
i really think that only IE's behaviour here matches author expectations
and i don't buy that it's 2 out of 4 :-)'"}"")
