conv_id=152674 | prompt="
CONVERSATION:
Something of interest that I don't see mentioned in that document is the rendering of hyphenated words (e.g. as a result of &amp;shy;).  Most indic scripts use the same hyphen character..." | raw="
CONVERSATION:
Something of interest that I don't see mentioned in that document is the rendering of hyphenated words (e.g. as a result of &amp;shy;).  Most indic scripts use the same hyphen character that latin script does; whereas Malayalam doesn't use any hyphenation marker at all when it splits a word over two lines.
I'm told that Hindi doesn't use hyphenation; so I'm not sure what to do if a &amp;shy; is encountered there.
There are a few other interesting hyphenation cases, too.  If I understand correctly, Arabic uses the tatweel/kashida character when hyphenating.  (I believe only at the end of the first line and not at the beginning of the second, though I'm not certain.)
Braille has a hyphen character; though a complication is that it can be used in the middle of a number, in which case the number prefix (sort of numlock character) needs to be repeated at the beginning of the next line.
Unicode has a character called &quot;Armenian hyphen&quot;, but i don't know whether that's to be used when splitting a word over two lines or if it's just for hyphenated phrases.  (E.g. Hebrew has a &quot;maqaf&quot; character that's described as being like a hyphen, but i think that's used only for things like hyphenated phrases rather than for splitting a word over two lines.)
8301d5dab3922cb1cb32aa2883838608: I'm not sure about the Arabic bit. The kashida character would look like it's extending the line if it's right after a joining character. But it doesn't look like that.
Similarly, I don't know whether Mongolian should use U+1806, and whether Katakana should use U+30fb.
ok.  I'll check with someone.
katakana doesn't hyphenate
it's allowed to break anywhere
with a hyphen glyph or without?
8301d5dab3922cb1cb32aa2883838608: http:/ 3f21e54c5c5ffd50fe3680acc025bb1d.inkedblade.net/style/scans/LoC025.png
8301d5dab3922cb1cb32aa2883838608: without
(sorry, that should have been obvious)
8301d5dab3922cb1cb32aa2883838608: Japanese does not hyphenate. Breaks are allowed everywhere, except before/after certain punctuation
8301d5dab3922cb1cb32aa2883838608: wrt that scan - The same character seems to be used in the middle of the line as a sort of dash
8301d5dab3922cb1cb32aa2883838608: I can't read the text, though, so I can't say for sure what it's doing :)
ah yes.  I had a comment in the source code about &quot;making sure it doesn't join&quot; re arabic, but I'd forgotten what i meant by that :)
8301d5dab3922cb1cb32aa2883838608: It might be that it's an actual hyphen, just the font renders it differently
8301d5dab3922cb1cb32aa2883838608: The dashes in the middle of the line appear to be longer than the ones at the end
that might not work well with font fallback stuff.
8301d5dab3922cb1cb32aa2883838608: so it's possible one's a hyphen, the other's a dash, and the font just adjusts the glyphs
8301d5dab3922cb1cb32aa2883838608: There's a lot of tweaks that have to be made as you render punctuation for different languages
8301d5dab3922cb1cb32aa2883838608: or other characters, even
8301d5dab3922cb1cb32aa2883838608: I don't think about this. No clear predatory indicators or concerning behavior were found. The conversation consists of 7 messages. First message: 'something of interest that I don't see mentioned in that document is the rendering of hyphenated words (e.g. as a result of &amp;shy;.  Most indic scripts use the same hyphen character that latin script does; however, so it could be used when splitting a word over two lines or if it's right after a join character.But it doesn't hyphenate. Breaks are allowed to break any hyphen.  Morell, but it's allowed to break any"")
