conv_id=154225 | prompt="
CONVERSATION:
hi all, i am trying to rotate an image inside a canvas by 90degrees, but everytime i rotate, the image i think goes out of the canvas visual area. any suggestions.
I checked out a stack..." | raw="
CONVERSATION:
hi all, i am trying to rotate an image inside a canvas by 90degrees, but everytime i rotate, the image i think goes out of the canvas visual area. any suggestions.
I checked out a stack overflow thread and it mentioned that the image needs to be translated, but i could not grasp why ?
Hi a02fb6d31a1fcd5cc2688643295ac550: Rotation in canvas actually rotates the 2d rendering contect within the canvas, not the shape that you're trying to draw.
Let me grab you a quick illustration :)
9a6651e9db26914297c1814554fd5ceb: ok
At the most basic level, this is what the canvas (2d rendering context) coordinate space looks like: http://cl.ly/5Xit
That's without any transformations, etc
When you rotate the canvas, it looks like this: http://cl.ly/5Y5h
So, imagine you drew a rectangle where the Y arrow is on the first image. When you rotate the canvas it would be drawn where the Y arrow is on the second image ‚Äì¬†outside of the visible canvas.
Does that make sense? (Will explain the fix once it does)
9a6651e9db26914297c1814554fd5ceb: just a moment. reading.
Take your time :)
9a6651e9db26914297c1814554fd5ceb: i understand now :)
Ok, so to fix that you need to translate the canvas *before* you draw your image
Translate just moves the (0, 0) point of the canvas, like so: http://cl.ly/5Xjo
You translate the canvas to the center of where you want to draw your image
Then rotate 90 degrees
Then draw the image with a minus (x, y) that is half the image width and height so that it appears in the center of the rotation
Sounds a bit crazy, but it works :)
9a6651e9db26914297c1814554fd5ceb: i should translate to the point about which i want to rotate ?
Yes, exactly that
9a6651e9db26914297c1814554fd5ceb: ok
Let me know if that works out for you
9a6651e9db26914297c1814554fd5ceb: trying to understand the minus(x,y) part.
Ok, that's cool (it's the weird part).
Basically, see that last image I posted?
When you translate, the (0, 0) point on the canvas (the origin) moves to the point that you translated to
So if you then drew a rectangle at the position (0, 0), it would appear at the translated position, not the top left like it would have before
Does that make sense?
9a6651e9db26914297c1814554fd5ceb: so if i drew the rectangle without the minus(x,y) it would appear translated and rotated about the center, but the translated image would not be aligned with the canvas right ?
If you drew it without the minus (x, y), the top left of the image would be at the point of rotation
Which would look a little odd
So the minus just drags it back half way on the (x, y) to put it on the center of the point of rotation
9a6651e9db26914297c1814554fd5ceb: i understand now.
Excellent :)
9a6651e9db26914297c1814554fd5ceb: just hang around for a while, i want to try it out with some arbitrary angles. hope you don't mind.
Go for it
If you want to learn in more detail, I wrote an entire book on learning canvas from back to front: http://www.amazon.co.uk/gp/product/1430232919/
:)
All of this kind of stuff is covered
(that's where those images came from)
9a6651e9db26914297c1814554fd5ceb: cool ! will check out the book.
It's not a general advertisement, I just think you might find it useful :)
9a6651e9db2ll.48514451488643195ac550:rotation in canvas actuallyrotting the 2d rendering contll within the canvas, but the translation text would not be aligned with the canvas right ?'. The conversation consists of 8 messages. First message: 'hi all, i am trying to rotate an image inside a canvas by 90degrees, but every time i rotate, the image i think go out of the canvas visual area. any suggestions.' Last message: 'It's not a general advertisement, I just think you might find it useful'"}"")
